From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 13:46:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 099/342] arm64 KDL: say that an interrupt's iframe has a stale
 ESR and FAR

Tested on the ODROID-C2 (+107) with busy threads holding known values
in x19 and v31: "bt" traced the two running on CPUs 0 and 1 from the
inter-CPU interrupt that halted them, through its iframe, into their
loop; "iframe -f" of the third showed its x19 and v31. The interrupt's
frame showed the last page fault's ESR (instruction abort), which
looked like the reason for the frame.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_debug.cpp | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_debug.cpp b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_debug.cpp
index 3063f0e38c..38adb21368 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_debug.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_debug.cpp
@@ -425,7 +425,6 @@ print_iframe(struct iframe* frame, bool fpu)
 		")  far %016" B_PRIx64 "\n", frame->esr, exceptionClass,
 		exception_class_name(exceptionClass), frame->esr & 0x1ffffff,
 		frame->far);
-		// an interrupt's frame has the last exception's ESR and FAR
 
 	if (!fpu)
 		return;
@@ -473,7 +472,8 @@ dump_iframes(int argc, char** argv)
 	struct iframe_stack* frameStack = thread != NULL
 		? &thread->arch_info.iframes : &gBootFrameStack;
 	if (thread != NULL) {
-		kprintf("iframes of thread %" B_PRId32 " \"%s\", the latest last\n",
+		kprintf("iframes of thread %" B_PRId32 " \"%s\", the latest last (an "
+			"interrupt's shows the ESR and FAR of the last exception)\n",
 			thread->id, thread->name);
 	}
 	if (frameStack->index == 0)
